## Migration Analysis

Your code has **3 patterns** that break on .NET 8:

| API | Pattern | Why it breaks |
|-----|---------|---------------|
| `Thread.Interrupt()` | Blocking call interruption | Obsolete (`SYSLIB0046` in .NET 9), unreliable |
| `Thread.Abort()` | Forcible termination | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |
| `ManualResetEvent.WaitOne()` | Non-cancellable block | Can't be woken by a `CancellationToken` |

There's also a **race condition** in the original: multiple `Enqueue` calls can signal `_newEvent` but `Reset()` + single `Dequeue()` loses items.

---

## Migrated Code (.NET 8)

```csharp
public class EventProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private readonly ManualResetEventSlim _newEvent = new(false);
    private Thread? _thread;
    private readonly object _lock = new();
    private readonly Queue<string> _queue = new();

    public void Start()
    {
        _thread = new Thread(RunLoop);
        _thread.Start();
    }

    private void RunLoop()
    {
        try
        {
            while (!_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // WaitAny wakes on either a new event OR cancellation
                WaitHandle.WaitAny(
                    new[] { _newEvent.WaitHandle, _cts.Token.WaitHandle });

                if (_cts.Token.IsCancellationRequested)
                    break;

                // Drain all queued items (fixes the original race condition)
                while (true)
                {
                    string? item;
                    lock (_lock)
                    {
                        if (_queue.Count == 0) break;
                        item = _queue.Dequeue();
                    }
                    Handle(item);
                }

                _newEvent.Reset();
            }
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException)
        {
            // Normal shutdown — nothing to do
        }
    }

    public void Enqueue(string item)
    {
        lock (_lock) { _queue.Enqueue(item); }
        _newEvent.Set();
    }

    public void Stop()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();              // signals the token → wakes WaitAny
        _thread?.Join(5000);        // wait up to 5s for graceful exit
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        Stop();
        _cts.Dispose();
        _newEvent.Dispose();
    }

    private void Handle(string item) { /* ... */ }
}
```

---

## Key Changes Explained

| Before (.NET Framework) | After (.NET 8) | Why |
|---|---|---|
| `ManualResetEvent` | `ManualResetEventSlim` | Lighter weight; same API |
| `_newEvent.WaitOne()` | `WaitHandle.WaitAny(event, token)` | Makes the block cancellation-aware |
| `_thread.Interrupt()` | `_cts.Cancel()` | Cooperative signal instead of injecting an exception |
| `_thread.Abort()` | *(removed)* | The loop exits naturally via token check |
| Single `Dequeue` | Drain loop | Fixes lost-event race in the original |
| No cleanup | `IDisposable` | Disposes `CancellationTokenSource` and wait handle |

---

## Pitfalls to Watch For

1. **`WaitOne()` alone ignores cancellation** — you *must* use `WaitHandle.WaitAny` with `_cts.Token.WaitHandle` or the thread will never wake on `Stop()`.

2. **Don't forget to dispose `CancellationTokenSource`** — it holds timer/callback resources. Implement `IDisposable`.

3. **Cancellation is cooperative, not instant** — if `Handle(item)` is long-running, it won't be interrupted mid-execution. Pass the token into `Handle` and check it periodically if items are expensive.

4. **Reset after drain, not before** — resetting the event before draining (as the original did) can lose signals from concurrent `Enqueue` calls.
